Preserve coordinate, elevation, and terrain-data meaning (@geo-coordinates: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/QGIS-Training-Manual.lingua#r_65XJEHNY3P1ESW5KF5V0871SFQ", #source "../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW", #source "../sources/SGB-Geodiversity-Methods.lingua#r_7QW5KYAYK9SB2MT3R6MNCFAQWK", #geology "Plan-and-perform-field-observation.lingua#r_3KBKETGY3QZKFDHPF0H0NAF5GA") { r_53936KRB4P2H90E3R2F9CNJRAJ
**Read:** [QGIS Training Manual — QGIS project](../sources/QGIS-Training-Manual.lingua#r_65XJEHNY3P1ESW5KF5V0871SFQ), the complete projection, georeferencing, vector, raster, and terrain modules used; [Geological Structures: a Practical Introduction — Waldron and Snyder](../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW), the orientation and map-geometry preparation; [Levantamento da geodiversidade — SGB technical guide, volume 5 version 4](../sources/SGB-Geodiversity-Methods.lingua#r_7QW5KYAYK9SB2MT3R6MNCFAQWK), cartographic/reference information and scale.

A coordinate needs a reference system, axis convention, units, and sometimes an epoch or transformation to locate an observation usefully. Geographic angular coordinates and projected metric coordinates require different operations. An elevation can refer to a vertical datum or another height convention; several apparent discrepancies can arise before any geology changes.

Distinguish map position, georeferencing, sensor resolution, interpolation, and interpretation uncertainty. A raster cell size is not automatically the accuracy of a terrain surface. A copied map image can carry a scale statement whose meaning changes if it is resized without preserving a spatial reference.

Use GIS to preserve and inspect relationships, not to hide the geometry behind tool names. Reprojection and assigning a missing coordinate system are different operations. Record the source, method, parameters, version, and output identity of an analysis, especially when combining map units, observations, and elevation data from different editions.

**Work:** complete the official projection/vector/raster exercises and inspect metadata for a selected authorized terrain/map source. Explain a deliberately mismatched reference-system overlay. Calculate one distance or gradient independently using a suitable local metric system and compare with the software result.

Use [Reproducible computational work](../../../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/Reproducible-computation.lingua#r_78FWWJDCV0ZGG7VYSPRCWN56DQ) for reproducible workflows and [Measurement and metrology](../../../../inquiry/metrology/Metrology.lingua#r_3G54ENPA974K19AB5XF0P641N5) for measurement meaning. Later original three-point calculations deliberately use a simple local Cartesian system with east, north, and up in metres; real coordinates need the transformations and metadata that make that model applicable.

